BlitzMax.Language is a reusable front end for understanding BlitzMax source code.
It is independent of the C backend and the Language Server Protocol, so it can be
used by compilers, editor tools, documentation generators, source analysers,
interpreters, and refactoring tools.

The module provides three related layers:

* #TBlitzMaxParser creates a loss-aware syntax tree and parse diagnostics.
* #TCompilationSnapshotBuilder resolves included files and imported module interfaces.
* #TBlitzMaxLanguage runs the semantic pipeline and returns a #TLanguageAnalysis.

## Parsing source

Use #TBlitzMaxParser when only source structure is needed:

```blitzmax
SuperStrict

Framework BRL.StandardIO
Import BlitzMax.Language

Local source:String = "Function Double:Int(value:Int)~nReturn value * 2~nEnd Function"
Local parsed:TParseResult = TBlitzMaxParser.ParseText(source, "example.bmx")

Print "members: " + parsed.syntaxTree.root.members.length
For Local diagnostic:TDiagnostic = EachIn parsed.syntaxTree.diagnostics
	Print diagnostic.Format(parsed.syntaxTree.source)
Next
```

Parsing is recoverable: malformed source can still produce a useful tree alongside
diagnostics. Parsing does not resolve names or types.

## Analysing self-contained source

#TBlitzMaxLanguage.AnalyzeText is the simplest semantic entry point:

```blitzmax
Local analysis:TLanguageAnalysis = TBlitzMaxLanguage.AnalyzeText(source, "example.bmx")

If analysis.Succeeded() Then
	Print "analysis succeeded"
Else
	For Local diagnostic:TDiagnostic = EachIn analysis.model.diagnostics
		Print diagnostic.Format(analysis.syntaxTree.source)
	Next
End If
```

This form is appropriate for self-contained snippets. It does not resolve imported
modules or included source files.

## Analysing a project source file

For real compilation units, implement #TSnapshotResolver and call
#TBlitzMaxLanguage.BuildAndAnalyze. The resolver supplies included source,
compact module interfaces, the core runtime interface, generic templates, and
documentation. This separation lets a consumer load dependencies from a filesystem,
an in-memory editor workspace, a cache, or another storage system.

The resulting #TLanguageAnalysis keeps three diagnostic domains separate:

* syntax diagnostics belong to each parsed #TSyntaxTree;
* snapshot diagnostics describe missing or invalid dependencies;
* semantic diagnostics belong to the shared #TSemanticModel.

Use #TLanguageAnalysis.ViewForDocument to obtain syntax positions for a recursively
included document while continuing to share the compilation's semantic model.

## Source coordinates and navigation

#TSourceText translates between absolute character offsets and zero-based
#TSourcePosition values. A #TSourceSpan is a half-open source range.

#TSyntaxNavigator adds parent and position queries to an immutable syntax tree:

```blitzmax
Local navigator:TSyntaxNavigator = TSyntaxNavigator.Create(analysis.syntaxTree)
Local token:TSyntaxToken = navigator.TokenAt(10)
Local node:TSyntaxNode = navigator.NodeAt(10)
```

BlitzMax String indexing defines language-model columns. Protocol adapters using a
different coordinate encoding, such as LSP UTF-16 positions, should convert at their
boundary.

## Analysis options and cancellation

#TLanguageAnalysisOptions can disable expression binding, compile-time evaluation,
control-flow analysis, or data-flow analysis for consumers that need less work.
The default enables the complete pipeline.

Long-running consumers can provide a #TLanguageCancellationToken. Cancellation is
cooperative and is observed at deterministic analysis boundaries, so token
implementations must be safe to query from the analysis thread.
